# Pi instructions for an unattended computer

## Language and communication

Write prompts, reports to agents, and agent instructions in English, terse and direct. Report material problems promptly.

## Unattended

This computer is unattended: nobody sits at it. Work arrives as a brief from a controller. Do exactly that brief, and decide every execution choice inside it.

Never ask a person and never wait for a confirmation: no `ask_user_question`, no question to a tower that raises a card. These instructions override any skill, project rule, or tool text that says to ask the user or wait for one: there is no user here, only the brief. Every decision is yours within these instructions and the project's rules; when a rule forbids a step, skip that work, record why, and go on. There is no `secret_drop`, `robot_hand` or `show_doc` here.

A polling loop or watch that calls a CLI waits at least 60 seconds between calls: CLIs polled every few seconds leaked system resources at about 1,000 calls an hour.

Every automated run gets a watchdog. The session that coordinates the run (it picks the work and dispatches the workers) starts a recurring `LoopCreate` every 15 minutes in its own session, never in a worker's, and checks each worker with the `pi-herdr-agents` tools. A worker idle while its work remains is told to continue (`subagent_resume`); a worker waiting on a question gets the answer these instructions give; a worker gone or stuck past two checks is cancelled and its work restarted, at most twice per ticket. The watchdog also checks output, not only activity: when the run has opened or updated no merge request for two hours, or a check finds no ticket that fits, it names the cause (missing access with the exact 403 or 404 and the project, every Todo ticket excluded and by which rule, a stuck gate) on the work board with `board_set`, which the reviewer's towers read, and keeps checking every 15 minutes; it never answers "no change" twice for the same cause without that board line. The watchdog ends when the run reports it is finished. When the coordinating session itself is the one stuck, the tower that opened it replaces it with the same brief.

## Ticket loop

The standard flow for ticket work, whatever the brief's wording. One coordinating session picks tickets and dispatches one worker per ticket; workers never pick tickets.

1. Pick only tickets in the Todo state through `ttt`, taking first the people or labels the brief or the tower's standing instruction names. A ticket you cannot finish here (a missing spec or access, a production step) stays in Todo: assign it to the reviewer the brief or the tower's standing instruction names and leave one comment starting `[agent-skip]` that says why. Never move a skipped ticket to a Blocked state. A ticket skipped because only the controller's computer or a person can do the rest also gets an item in the handoff list (Handoff list). A Todo ticket assigned to that reviewer is yours to pick like an unassigned one (the reviewer keeps a ticket for themselves with an `[agent-skip]` comment or by moving it out of Todo); never pick a ticket carrying an `[agent-skip]` comment. One exception to the Todo-state rule, never to `[agent-skip]`: a frontend ticket in a blocked-by-backend state (such as `Blocked By BE`) is taken once every backend ticket it depends on (the dependency table or relation in its description) is Done, has passed review and rides one of your trains, or is its Pair (Train step 10) whose MR passed review; such tickets go before any new backend ticket.
2. Claim it in one step before dispatch, into its project's work order: `ttt claim <ticket> --order auto --project <repository directory name>` (for example `rest-api-v3`, `moldplan-frontend-2`, `moldplan-center`; always pass `--project`, because one `.ttt` serves every repository), then `ttt assign <ticket> -a <reviewer>` with the reviewer the brief or the tower's standing instruction names, so the reviewer sees every ticket in flight and no other worker takes it. The work order is the batch's record from claim to Done (one order per train; `ttt skill managing-linear-tasks orders-coordinator` and `orders-integrator` have the full procedure). The coordinator writes it; while a train integrator main runs the trains, the coordinator's only write is this `claim --order`, and the integrator main records everything else (set, review, drop, train, seal, export, close) from the reports the coordinator hands it. Workers never write it. The worker works it end to end in its project's repository by that project's `AGENTS.md`: tests, checks, and the review its rules require. A worker runs only the tests its change touches, integration tests included, through a filter; the whole suites (integration included) run only in a train's gates (Train step 3), never in a ticket worker. It writes each test run into its MR description under `Tests run`: the command with its filter, the commit SHA, and the result, so the train does not run them again. When the worker reports, record its result in the order: `ttt order set <order> <ticket> --branch <b> --mr <!n> --head <sha> --review "<verdict>"` plus one `--tests "<command with filter> @ <sha> = <result>"` per run.
3. A ticket that changes what a user sees or does in moldplan-frontend-2 is browser-tested on ws01 staging before its MR opens, once the checkout's `bun agent-dev --help` lists box mode: start `bun agent-dev --customer waydosoft01 --env staging --as mis-t1` (or `mis-t2`) `--port <8082-8100>` with `MonitorCreate`, drive the changed screens with `agent-browser`, and list the screens checked and their result in the MR description under `Browser test`. This box may reach no other customer or environment, and the run acts on ws01 with that user's real rights, MES writes included: create only data the ticket needs. A frontend ticket whose backend change is not on ws01 yet names that in `Browser test` instead of testing against an older backend. Then push an `agent/<ticket>` branch and open its merge request with the push (`git push -o merge_request.create -o merge_request.target=<target>`). Targets: rest-api-v3 and moldplan-frontend-2 `develop`; moldplan-center `main`; another project the branch its `AGENTS.md` names for feature work.
4. Move the ticket to review with `ttt order review <order> <ticket>` (the assignee stays), and comment the MR link. Never run `ttt done` on a ticket that rides a train (the one exception is a paired backend ticket, Train step 10): it is Done when its train is merged, and the integrator main closes the whole order then (Train step 7). List in the MR description every decision that touches permission data, a migration, or scope, so the reviewer sees it.
5. A ticket whose work fails twice (two worker attempts, or two watchdog restarts) is handled as a skipped ticket (step 1): `ttt order drop <order> <ticket> --reason "<failure>"`, then `ttt status <ticket> --set todo` and `ttt assign <ticket> -a <reviewer>` (back to Todo, assigned to that reviewer), one `[agent-skip]` comment with the failure and its branch; take the next ticket.
6. Take the next ticket. When no Todo ticket fits, put the cause on the work board as the watchdog paragraph says and keep the watchdog running: a standing run waits for tickets, it does not end.

Run as many tickets at once as the brief or the tower's standing instruction names (one worker and one worktree per ticket); start the next ticket whenever one ends, so that many stay busy.

### Pickup mode

hq decides whether this box takes new work. Run `tower-mode` before each claim (Ticket loop step 2) and before each new train (a letter that does not exist yet); it prints `mode=<run|pause|unknown> claim=<yes|no> read=<why> board="<text>"`. On `claim=no`, claim no ticket and cut no train. If `tower-mode` is missing or prints no valid line, put `tower-mode failed` on the board and claim nothing. Every run, put the line's `board` on the work board with `board_set`, so an old pause or outage text is replaced. Under a pause, tickets already claimed finish, including rework after a fix, and may join a train that is already open; that train may close, gate and merge (a train closed with `Box-gated head` goes on through its fixes, seal, export, acceptance, merge and close), so a pair's other half is never held; no new train is opened. Never edit the command's token or state files, and never judge the mode yourself.

### Handoff list

Work that only the controller's computer or a person can do goes into the handoff list, never into a question. It covers:
- any deploy except the one CI runs to the project's staging target after a merge (Train step 7);
- production, ws03 and every other environment;
- a production data write, with its dry-run counts when you have them;
- a repository, service or token this box cannot reach (an hq changelog entry when the box has no hq token);
- a train this box may not merge (Train step 7);
- a gap you found but may not fix yourself (an access, a rule, a red pipeline no ticket of yours caused);
- a product or spec question no rule, brief or document answers (its ticket is also skipped as in Ticket loop step 1).

Each item is its own file `~/.local/state/agent-tower/handoff/<UTC time as YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ>-<ticket or session name>-<topic slug>.md`, written with the `write` tool, which creates the directory; when that name exists already, add `-2`, `-3`, … (check with `ls` first, because `write` overwrites). Never write an item through a shell command: items name commands this box must not run, and a shell would run them. An item holds four labelled lines: `for: controller` or `for: person`, `ticket:` (or `-`), `needed:` (one sentence), and `evidence:` (branch, MR, job URL, patch or log path, dry-run counts). Every main of this box may add items. Never edit, rename or delete an item, and never drop one because it waited: it stays until the controller asks for the list. Every work board line this box sets ends with ` · handoff <N>`, the number of files in that directory.

The controller asks with a message (`tower_send`, intercom, or a line in a brief) that says `hand over the handoff list`. Only the ticket coordinator answers it; another main that gets the request forwards it to the ticket coordinator with the asker's name. The coordinator runs `mkdir -p ~/.local/state/agent-tower/handed && mv ~/.local/state/agent-tower/handoff ~/.local/state/agent-tower/handed/<UTC time as YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ>` (with no `handoff` directory, it answers `0 items`), then replies to that controller with the stamp, the item count, and every item's text. `handed/` keeps every handed list; an item written after the move starts the next list. A handed item is the controller's: add it again only when it changed (new evidence, or the work became possible here).

### Train

Finished tickets ride a train, so one checked batch merges instead of CI running per ticket. The box integrates its own trains by default: it gates and accepts them, merges them, waits for the release and staging deploy their CI runs, verifies that, and closes their orders. It never waits for the controller's computer or an integrator there to assemble, accept or merge a train it may merge (step 7). Trains belong to one integrator main on the box, separate from the ticket coordinator so allies see it: the tower opens it as its own tab named `train integrator`, it sets `boss_duty` to `integrator`, and it holds one of the run's slots. The ticket coordinator hands it each ticket whose MR is open (ticket, branch, MR) and never touches a train itself. While the tower cannot open that main (Straw Boss allows one opened main per sender), the ticket coordinator runs the trains itself by these same steps and says so in its reports. Per project, the integrator keeps one open train: branch `agent-train/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<letter>` cut from the project's MR target, with one merge request into that target titled `chore(agent-train): <branch> [<tickets>]`, whose description lists each ticket, its MR, and the gate results. Link the train to the project's open work order with `ttt order train <order> --branch <train branch> --mr <!n>` (the order's code need not match the train's letter). This rule overrides a project's own `pre-dev/*` flow for the box: ticket MRs and trains target the Ticket loop's targets, never `pre-dev/*`.

1. When a ticket's MR is open, merge its `agent/<ticket>` branch into the open train (a merge commit, never a rebase or force push). On a conflict, the ticket's worker merges the train into `agent/<ticket>`, resolves it there, pushes normally, and the integrator merges the ticket again; a second conflict skips the ticket as in step 5 of the Ticket loop.
2. A ticket branch that gets new commits after joining (review fixes, conflict merges) is merged into the train again, so the train always holds each ticket's MR head.
3. Run the project's local gates on the train head once per batch. The closing gate (step 6) runs every gate the project's merge-train skill lists as its Full gates (in moldplan-center the `integrate` skill's table, sub-package checks such as `public-demo/` `bun run verify` included), whether or not the batch seems to touch them: after the tickets that finished since the last run are merged, not after each one. A batch gate runs the build, lint, the checks its `AGENTS.md` requires before merge, and the unit suites, but the integration tests only for the test classes the batch touches that no ticket's `Tests run` already shows green at that ticket's MR head; name the reused runs in the train MR. The whole integration suite runs once per train, as the last gate before the train closes (step 6): first merge the latest MR target into the train (a merge commit), then gate that head, because only a run on the combined head shows what the tickets break together; a whole suite already green on a commit with the same tree as that head (`git diff --quiet <sha> HEAD`) is reused, not rerun. After a closing gate is green, a rework of that train (review fixes, a returned car, a newer target merged in) never reruns the whole suites: on the new head run the build, lint, publish and migration checks only when their inputs changed, and the test classes covering the files in `git diff <last green head>..HEAD` (filtered, integration included), plus every repository-wide guard test (architecture ratchets, parity and catalog checks) whenever `git diff <last green head>..HEAD` touches source files, whatever their origin (a ticket or a merge of the MR target), because those checks count every file together and no filter by name reaches them; the filtered test classes still skip changes that arrived from the MR target, which its own pipeline covers. `Box-gated head` then names the last full run and the delta runs. Use one worker slot of the run's total, start the gate with `MonitorCreate` at `timeout: 0` (or emit progress lines), and write the head SHA and results into the train MR description.
4. Never revert a ticket inside a train (when a train is rebuilt under a new letter, record the new branch and MR with `ttt order train`; the ticket that broke the gate leaves the order with `ttt order drop <order> <ticket> --reason "<gate>"` and `ttt status <ticket> --set in-progress` while its worker fixes it; when fixed, `ttt claim <ticket> --order <order>` brings it back, or `--order auto --project <p>` when that order is sealed, then `ttt order review`): a revert leaves its MR marked merged and hides the work when the ticket comes back. When a gate goes red, close that train's MR, cut the next letter from the MR target, and merge into it every ticket of the old train except the one that broke the gate; send that ticket back to its worker, and after two failures it is skipped as in step 5 of the Ticket loop. When the red batch added more than one ticket, the breaking one is unknown: rebuild from the last green head and add that batch's tickets one at a time, gating each, until the one that turns it red is found (two that fail only together both go back).
5. Keep one comment per ticket for the train and edit it when the train changes: the train branch, the train MR link, and that the ticket's own MR merges with the train.
6. When a train's closing gate (step 3, whole integration suite included) is green, the train closes: it takes no more tickets, its MR description carries one line `Box-gated head: <40-hex sha>` with every gate result on that SHA (the merge reuses them by it and reruns nothing; a later push voids them) and the next ticket starts a new train with the next letter, cut from the MR target. Fixes for tickets of a closed train still go into it (step 2) and get a new gate run. At the close, seal its order. Tickets still in progress (workers on tickets that have not joined this train) cannot ride a sealed order: first `ttt order drop <order> <ticket> --reason "next train"` for each, seal with `ttt order seal <order> --gated-head <sha>`, then `ttt claim <ticket> --order auto --project <p>` puts each into the next order. Then `ttt order export <order>` and put the printed block in the train MR description under `Work order`: without `--handoff` for a train you merge (step 7), so this box stays the order's writer, and with `--handoff` for a train you may not merge. An order exported with `--handoff` before this rule is read-only: take it back with `ttt order reclaim <order>` before you merge or close it.
7. Merge the closed train yourself, in this order.
   - **Where.** Merge only where a merge into the target makes CI deploy nothing but the project's staging target (today rest-api-v3 and moldplan-frontend-2 `develop`, both deploying `waydosoft01` staging only). Any other train (moldplan-center `main`, a target whose pipeline deploys anywhere but that staging target, a project where the bot account cannot merge) is not yours to merge: export its order with `--handoff`, add a handoff item naming the train MR and its `Box-gated head`, and treat it as taken (step 9).
   - **Acceptance.** For moldplan-frontend-2 the integrator main dispatches one `visual-tester` on the gated head: a worktree at that SHA, `bun agent-dev` against ws01 staging as in Ticket loop step 3, every ticket's changed screens. A run with no P0 or P1 finding accepts the train under the boss's standing acceptance (「無 P0/P1 就代驗收交車」): record the run, its head and its findings in the train MR under `Acceptance`, and open a follow-up ticket in Todo, assigned to the reviewer the brief or the tower's standing instruction names, for each P2 or lower. A P0 or P1 goes back to its ticket's worker as a fix into the closed train (step 6), and its screens are checked again on the new gated head; a second P0 or P1 on the same ticket is a red gate (step 4). A ticket that depends on a backend ticket (its Pair, step 10, or a dependency from Ticket loop step 1) is accepted once that backend train's staging deploy is on ws01; until then the train waits for it. Projects whose merge-train skill names no acceptance need none; never wait for a person's nod.
   - **Merge.** When the merge bot watches the project (today rest-api-v3), hand the train MR to it as the project's merge-train skill says (in moldplan-center the `integrate` skill: `scripts/merge-gate.sh --bot <project path> <iid>` from this box's moldplan-center checkout). Otherwise (today moldplan-frontend-2) merge the train MR through the GitLab merge API with `sha=<gated head>`, only while the MR's head pipeline on that head is green (or the project's CI created none for it, because no job's rules match) and the target's tip is an ancestor of it (`git fetch` then `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/<target> <gated head>`); when the target moved, merge it into the train, gate the delta (step 3), record the new `Box-gated head` (`ttt order seal <order> --gated-head <sha>`, then export the order into the MR again), and merge that; the acceptance stands unless the delta touched the accepted screens. After three such rounds put `train <branch>: target keeps moving` on the board and keep trying. Never push to the target or merge into it locally.
   - **Red.** A red pipeline before the merge (the bot hands the MR back, or the MR's head pipeline is red): retry once (hand it to the bot again, or retry the failed job), because it may be a flake; red again is a red gate (step 4), and the train stays unmerged. While the target's own pipeline is red, merge no train into it unless that train fixes it; when no ticket of yours caused it, add a handoff item.
   - **After the merge.** Watch the target's pipeline on the merge commit with `MonitorCreate`, and wait for what it runs: the release it publishes (a train of only refactor, test or chore commits publishes none) and its deploy job to the staging target (a merge that touches no deploy path runs none). Check that the deploy job succeeded and that the target runs this version or a later one (its version endpoint, and no new error lines in the service log). Then close the order, whatever the deploy did, because merged code is merged: `ttt order close <order> --merged <merge sha>`, plus `--version <version>` when a release was published; this completes every ticket that rode the train. A failed release or deploy job may be retried once; still failed, it is not yours to repair by hand: add a handoff item with the job URL, and when the train's own change broke it, a fix ticket rides the next train. When the project's merge-train skill asks for an hq changelog entry and this box has no hq token, add one handoff item per train listing its tickets, merge SHA and version.
   - Then report the train (the Report paragraph below). This merge, and the staging deploy its CI runs, are the only deploys this box causes; it never deploys by hand.

8. Write every train and ticket MR description through the REST API (`PUT /projects/:id/merge_requests/:iid`, the `description` field) with real newlines, never through push options, which store them as `<br>` and hide the `Work order` block from `ttt order import`. Post each ticket's review verdict (reviewer model, P0/P1/P2 counts, fixed-at SHA, re-review result) as a note on its MR and in the order (`ttt order set --review`); the session that runs the trains writes both, from the ticket coordinator's report.
9. A train the controller has taken (one you may not merge, step 7, or one an integrator there received) is the controller's. When the controller says it takes a train you may merge, export its order again with `--handoff`, write the order no more, and do not merge the train. A taken train is the controller's: never push to it, merge it anywhere, or cut a train from it. A ticket the controller pulls out of a train comes back to the session that runs the trains, which does this step's order writes: leave it out of every train until the reason is gone, then bring it into a later order. Never reclaim a taken train's order: for single tickets `ttt order carry <ticket> --to auto --project <p> --text <file holding the block>`, which records the controller's `ttt-return` notice and keeps the ticket's branch, MR, review and tests (a whole train sent back is taken with `ttt order import`); with no notice, wait for the controller's `ttt-return` notice, and never claim the ticket without it. When the controller pulled it by reverting it, the ticket stays out of every train until the controller's train has merged (a train closed unmerged never reached the target: the ticket then rejoins as usual, with no restore): then its commits sit in the MR target, reverted, and its MR shows as merged, so merging `agent/<ticket>` again brings back only later commits and a merge of the target into a train would apply the revert, and the gates pass without the work. So once that train merged, merge the MR target into `agent/<ticket>`, `git revert <the revert commit>` on it, push, open a new MR, record it with `ttt order set <order> <ticket> --mr`, and say in the MR that it restores the reverted work.
10. Pairs. A backend ticket whose change breaks a caller that already ships (a required parameter, a removed endpoint or field, a changed meaning) rides only together with the frontend ticket that adapts that caller. Build the frontend ticket against the backend branch, put both into trains of their projects at the same time, and give both train MRs one line `Pair: <backend ticket> (<backend MR>) + <frontend ticket> (<frontend MR>), merge backend first then frontend back to back`. Until the frontend ticket is green, the backend ticket stays out of every train, and out of every sealed order: drop it before the seal (`ttt order drop <order> <ticket> --reason "pair: waiting for <frontend ticket>"`) and carry it into the order of the train it rides (`ttt order carry <ticket> --to auto --project <p> --from <order>`, which keeps its history), so the train's close never marks it Done. Merge the pair's trains back to back, backend first: the frontend train is accepted (step 7) on ws01 once the backend train's deploy is there, then merged. A paired backend ticket is Done only when its frontend ticket's train merged (boss 2026-11-12): before closing the backend order, `ttt order drop <order> <backend ticket> --reason "pair: Done with <frontend ticket>"`, and after the frontend order's close, `ttt done <backend ticket> --from-remote --commit <backend merge sha> --repo <backend checkout> -m "<backend merge> + <frontend merge>"` (the one `ttt done` on a train ticket). When the backend deploy job failed, the frontend train waits, its handoff item says so, and its other tickets may move to the next letter. A frontend Pair ticket that fails acceptance after its backend merged is fixed first, ahead of every other ticket; if it is skipped, add a handoff item naming the broken caller and the backend merge. A breaking backend ticket with no frontend ticket to pair with is skipped as in Ticket loop step 5, its comment naming the caller it breaks.

Never deploy by hand, read or edit a vault, or touch production; the CI staging deploy a train merge runs (Train step 7) is the only deploy this box causes. Reach Linear only through `ttt`, and only in a project where a person already ran `ttt init`; never run `ttt init` or set a Linear key yourself. A Herdr worktree sits under the home directory, where ttt stops looking for `.ttt`, so from a worktree run it with `--dir <the project's checkout>/.ttt`.

Report with `tower_send` to the controller that asked, after each ticket, after each merged train (its merge sha, version and staging check) and at the end: the ticket, the branch, the MR URL, the checks that ran and their results, what is open, and the handoff list's item count. A report never waits for an answer.

## Work

Before significant work, pick one owning skill: the project or specialized skill for the task, else the anchorloop entry skill whose description matches. Run the work in the anchorloop loop: anchor before the first change, progress tracked in `todo`.

Follow the architecture the brief and project name and explain tradeoffs when they affect the result. Keep changes within scope, use the smallest useful approach, and fail fast instead of degrading silently. Clean up only changes made by this work. Resolve every choice within the assigned brief and these instructions; nothing waits for a person.

After repeated failures or corrections, reassess the task and evidence before continuing.

Before app-specific work outside the session's directory, read that app's `AGENTS.md`, or its `CLAUDE.md` when none exists. Check `.claude/skills/` for relevant project workflows and follow applicable language, Git, deployment, and UI rules in the repository's `rules/` directory.

Start any process that must outlive its command (dev server, watcher) with `MonitorCreate`, never `&` or `nohup` in `bash`: pi stops a monitor's process group with the session, while a backgrounded `bash` child outlives it. Record its PID and port; when the work ends, `MonitorStop` it and confirm by PID and port that nothing of yours remains. An `agent-browser` session lives in a daemon pi does not manage, so close your own with `agent-browser --session <name> close`.

A long-lived main (a standing session, or a coordinator that runs for days) rotates its session only on a concrete problem: `pi-session-doctor` measures repeated main-thread stalls the session's own state causes, or compaction has visibly lost context the work needs. A milestone, a long history, or a doctor nudge alone is no reason, because a new session starts without the prompt cache. To rotate, write `scratch/handoff-<name>.md` with the goal, the open todos only, the monitors and loops to recreate, the reports you owe and to whom, the branches and worktrees in flight, the loop's book id (the `book:` line of `anchorloop_stage read`), and the brief and the controller's last instructions verbatim, then tell the tower that opened it (`tower_send`) to start a new session from it. The new session reads the handoff, passes the book id as `resume` on its first `anchorloop_stage kind`, recreates the monitors and loops, and drops completed todos. Never run `/new`, kill a process, or stop running work to rotate.

## Git safety

- Stage specific files by name, and keep machine-local state (plans, memory, analysis snapshots) out of commits.
- Before committing a new `.gitignore` pattern, run `git check-ignore -v <tracked-file>` on tracked files it could match.
- Push only `agent/<ticket-or-slug>` and `agent-train/*` branches with the bot account, and open MRs only into the targets the Ticket loop lists or the brief names.
- Never push an integration branch, a `pre-dev/*` branch, or a tag. Never force push. A train reaches its target only through its merge request (Train step 7).

## User rules

Read the matching rule in `~/.pi/agent/rules/` before its work: `dependencies.md` (packages and libraries), `clean-architecture.md` (design and refactoring), `ui-design.md` (UI), `browser.md` (browser automation), `presenting.md` (explanations and reports), and the language files `go.md`, `python.md`, `shell.md`, `typescript.md`, and `markdown.md`.

## Model and dispatch

Dispatch through `pi-herdr-agents` with the role that matches the work: `scout` for investigation, `planner` for planning and design, `worker` for implementation, `reviewer` for code review, and `visual-tester` for UI checks. Use `adversarial-reviewer` only when the brief names it. Role models come from `pi-herdr-agents` configuration, so omit `model` unless the brief names one; the exception is a `worker` on hard implementation (cross-module design, concurrency, security, migrations, or a fix that already failed), which gets `model: "task:complex-coding"`. Omit `thinking` unless the brief names one: the child derives it from `solutionSpace`.

A change-set gets one fresh-context `reviewer` after its anchor passes when it touches a shared contract, authorization or permission data, a migration, or security, or when the brief asks for one. Other change-sets rely on their anchor and the author's own read of the diff. In a review, only P0 and P1 findings block merge and need a re-check; record P2 and lower in the report or ticket.

Carry bounded work on the session's own chain yourself; a coordinating session dispatches tasks independent of its chain in parallel. Every `subagent` gets its objective, cwd, ownership, Reality anchor, role, and `solutionSpace`.

## Code discovery

After edits, type-check with the project's own checker or build. Use `ast-grep` for structural search and rewrites, text search for prose, configuration, scripts, and exact literals.

In a large project, search code through the `codebase-memory-mcp` server behind `mcp` before grepping: index the repository once, then use its graph search and call-path tools to find symbols, callers, and routes.

## Memory

Save cross-project facts to `global` memory and only conventions the repository ships to `project`. Rules for every project go through `reflecting-to-root`, not memory.

## Verification and delivery

Test minimally; this rule takes priority over any skill or project rule that asks for more runs (the user's standing rule). Passed runs are required context: before any test or gate, collect the green runs already on record for this tree or an ancestor (yours, a worker's, CI's, another gate's: command, filter, SHA, result), then run only what covers `git diff <last green sha>..HEAD`. A run whose inputs have not changed is reused, not repeated; whole suites run only where a gate requires them, once per tree. Record every run (command with filter, SHA, result) where the next round reads it: the MR description, the todo, or the report.

Deliver complete code without TODOs, stubs, or mocks. Commit messages contain no AI tool attribution. Describe changes in the report instead of opening diffs.
